  "full_response": "To create a sample SSH config file that references private key paths and includes comments with associatedphrases for each key, follow the structure below. This example assumes you have multiple private keys stored in the default SSH `~/.ssh/` directory, with filenames indicative of their purpose (e.g., `personal_id_rsa`, `wrk_xx_id_rsa`), and that you've encrypted them with a passphrase.\n\n```ssh\n# SSH Configuration File Sample (known as ~/.ssh/config)\n\n# Personal key with passphrase \"changeit\"\nHost *\n  IdentityFile ~/.ssh/personal_id_rsa\n  PasswordAuthentication no\n  PubkeyAuthentication yes\n  # Note the passphrase for personal_id_rsa: changeit\n\n# Work server 1\nHost work_server_1\n  HostName work1.example.com\n  User work_user1\n  IdentityFile ~/.ssh/wrk_xx_id_rsa\n  PasswordAuthentication no\n  PubkeyAuthentication\n  # Note the passphrase for wrk_xx_id_rsa: WrkPassMe123!\n\n# Work server 2, another project\nHost work_server_2\n  HostName work2.example.com\n  User work_user2\n  IdentityFile ~/.ssh/work_project_2_id_rsa\n  PasswordAuthentication no  PubkeyAuthentication yes\n  # Note the passphrase for work_project_2_id_r: ProjectPass$$123!\n\n# SSH connection for everyday use\nHost myserver\n  HostName myserver.example.com\n  User myuser\n  IdentityFile ~/.ssh/regular_rsa\n  PasswordAuthentication no\n  PubkeyAuthentication yes\n  # Note the passphrase for regular_id_rsa: EverydayPass1!\n```\n\nKey Points:\n- **Host Name**: You can change the host name in every line (except for the '*' wildcard, which is a catch-all that references all hosts) to match the Hostname of the server you want to connect with.\n- **User**: Specify the appropriate username for the server you're connecting to.\n- **IdentityFile**: Adjust this path to point your private key based on how you've stored it.\n- **PasswordAuthentication and PubkeyAuthentication**: `no` for `PasswordAuthentication` and `yes` for `PubkeyAuthentication` ensure key-based authentication. The alternate key (your private key file) is passed without a password, while the public key is required.\n\nThe comments at the bottom of each `Host` entry serve",
